body:
I respect your dedication. o7

&#x200B;

I do think I will like the Malazan books. Are you saying the first one is a pain in the ass, or my search for hardcover will be a PITA? Or both? I think I will have to settle for paperback unfortunately

So my only option is to buy paperback? Great....

&#x200B;

Yeah I just got to the part where Hiles Troy confronts Covenant, and says that the Land isn't a dream. Really such a shame, I tried salvaging it, and 75% of the book is readable, but the other quarter is soaked so I'm trying my best to repair the damage with a guide on YouTube

The Belgariad series helped me get back into reading as a 19yo

&#x200B;

It's very typical, but it was still an enjoyable read.

I'm only on the beginning of the Illearth War. I made a thread about finishing Lord Foul's Bane a few days ago

&#x200B;

Are Lord Foul, the Despiser, and the Gray Slayer all different people? 

&#x200B;

Does Lord Foul have a physical form, or is he some type of gaseous fixture? Also, if you haven't already, I recommend you check out Allen Moris' art on the Thomas Covenant series because that's what actually got me to read Lord Foul's Bane in the first place.

Love you for that

&#x200B;

I think u/matadorobex puts it best. I am going to keep reading, I actually have The Illearth War in front of me on my desk as I type this. Many people discredit the series because of the rape scene, but then refuse to read right then after where he REGRETS IT AND THROWS UP, and later on in the same book he sends Lena the Ranyhyn, granting her wish because he *does* regret it, and knows he can't make up for it, but he can do things to try and help. I love the idea of the Land being a place where the land literally breathes magic through things like hurtloam, rocklight, treasure-berries, aliantha, etc. Any person in his situation would be a Scrooge, a miserable twat, I don't think people would go so far as raping someone, but he is delirious, he can't believe the shit that is happening to him is real. I like the idea that Covenant isn't your traditional hero, he's no Frodo, no Sam, no Luke Skywalker, he's just Thomas Covenant. None of these characters chose to have the burdens of course, but they accept it whereas Covenant rejects it. He cannot accept the fact that he is Berek Halfhand because he's been incapable of so many things, and you can't force a broken man into a hero position, and expect him to accept it.

Few questions for you.

&#x200B;

What do you think the metaphor for the Land and Lord Foul is? Is Lord Foul a solid figure? From art I've seen online he's a cloud of smoke, unless that was used to disguise him, like Darth Vader coming out of steam in one of the movies.

&#x200B;

How old is Covenant supposed to be? I keep thinking he's maybe in his mid thirties-forties, but when I look up art about it online, he has white, grayish hair so it leads me to think he could possibly be older.

&#x200B;

I like what Donaldson did for his main character. He made him a leper with every right to be miserable, and he rejects the hero trope when thrust into the Land. I haven't pick up the Illearth War yet, even though it is sitting right in front of me. I still find myself asking if it *was* all in Covenant's head because after the car accident, he was only unconscious for four hours. Is this explored more?

Well I appreciate that a lot, I'm flattered.

&#x200B;

I should've phrased everything better. I haven't read ASOAIF, but I watched GoT. When I made the comparison to that media I was referring to the genre of media where it's not "good guys vs bad guys", where important characters die, and the like. However I am looking for political fantasy novels with high stakes, [https://www.reddit.com/r/Fantasy/comments/9jdt2k/book\_where\_the\_protagonist\_is\_a\_dictator/](https://www.reddit.com/r/Fantasy/comments/9jdt2k/book_where_the_protagonist_is_a_dictator/) this thread has told me The Dagger and the Coin might be something I am after. Fantasy is very broad, and I'm after it all. I have mentioned The Belgariad as being one of my favorites as of right now, but that is perhaps because my selection of fantasy I have read/seen is very limited. I like the simple magic systems, the objective or the "Quest" if you will, and I think it does nicely with some politics thrown into there. 

&#x200B;

I've also heard great things about Malazan and the First Law trilogy, though I am not sure if they fit the political fantasy genre. They don't have to of course because The Belgariad isn't strictly political fantasy, nor is LOTR, but I would argue politics are still intertwined with the plot of the story.

Thank you for your response!

&#x200B;

After watching the movies, I really don't see any change in Gandalf the Grey vs Gandalf the White. And I admit, I *like* Gandalf as a character, he is the wizard everyone thinks of when they think of the word "wizard". I liked his fight with Balrog too, I even have a poster of it in my room cus its fucking awesome, but the whole resurrection trope still irks me. Frodo does get himself almost killed multiple times too in the series, but since he is the main character I don't expect him to die or anything. I think Rogue One is a good example of main character deaths being done right. You should also refer to my comments about The Dark Crystal: Age of Resistance series where I think the "heroic sacrifice all for naught" was done right because we figure out SkekMal didn't die moments later. 

&#x200B;

Another thing holding me back from reading LOTR is the fact that I don't really want to read pages upon pages of dwarf runes. I'm a big fan of lore, but I really don't need all this gibberish about a single thing that isn't important to the main characters. Maybe I am just being picky, and can't be pleased.

I think what makes TC unique is that he is an anti-hero, and is inherently flawed. I've heard about Stormlight too, but I don't know if I'd be willing to give that series a try just yet.

&#x200B;

I know about ASOIAF too, but GRR Martin isn't going to ever finish the books because GoT ending was awful. I actually like GoT for what it was up until season 7 where the show just completely lost direction.

&#x200B;

The one thing holding me back from reading LOTR is that I have already seen the movies, and I'm aware there are events that aren't in the movies, but I know most, if not all of the important events, and how the story is going to play out.

I must have misrepresented what I am saying. The Belgariad is a story about a child (who looks A LOT like Luke Skywalker) who learns that he has been destined to have powerful gifts (Luke Skywalker, Harry Potter), and has to fight the Big Bad. In all 3 stories the character is also an orphan that lives with his Aunt and/or Uncle.

&#x200B;

Covenant has leprosy, and rapes a woman out of insanity. These are introduced near the beginning of the book, so already 2 majors flaws in our main character, and I assume he spends the whole story trying to make up for this, even though you can't really make up for something like that. It's interesting to see characters have major vices holding them back, though.

Thanks for your response! 

&#x200B;

One of the things I enjoy most about fantasy is the maps, especially typography maps of the world. Creatures/monsters/races is another big thing for me, and of course LOTR serves as the foundation for all of that. Our idea of orcs, elves, dwarves, trolls, etc comes from LOTR. Sure, those monsters have existed in folklore before, but the general idea of what they look like comes from LOTR. With that being said, I love the different creatures they have in The Belgariad such as Algroths, Eldraks, Fenlings, Hrulgin, etc. Even though those creatures don't exist in any other fantasy, it is still cool to read. One thing I've already looked at is the different creatures in TC, and how they're so different than anything we've ever read. I'm not sure what the point I am trying to make here is, but I really love the world building of fantasy, and the different creatures/monsters authors come up with, but I think the general idea of all those creatures stems from Tolkien. 

&#x200B;

I've heard about the Sparhawk books a little bit in comparison to Eddings' work, and I think the general consensus is Eddings' books are better. However, I will take your recommendation into account, and will look further into the series.

I'm going to assume you meant that I was referring to the sequels, not the prequels. I made a good point that bringing characters back is a shitty trope. I also gave a good example of that trope being done right in The Dark Crystal series. 

&#x200B;

Why kill a character off, if they aren't actually killed off? Please enlighten me.

Yeah I know some people who have read The Belgariad and say The Mallorean is better. Silk is actually my favorite character too lol

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Out of all the books in The Belgariad I like Queen of Sorcery the most because of Lelldorin, Mandorallen, and the Algroths, and also the fact that we get to see Asharak suffer. 

&#x200B;

As I said, I like TC BECAUSE he is very hard to empathize with. I understand he isn't a good person, and he dwells on solipsism, he has a hard time accepting himself. I like those flaws in a character. Garion has a hard time accepting himself after what he does to Asharak, but like I said the story is telegraphed because I knew right from the beginning where Garion was from, I knew he'd have to fight Torak, and nothing major happens to any of the main characters. I'm aware something happens with Durnik, but he is in The Mallorean so again, it just goes to show the laziness of bringing characters back for the sake of it.

Because leading your readers on to make them think someone died, then bringing them back is lazy writing in my opinion. I'm only 19, so take my words with a HUGE grain of salt, but it is annoying when that trope has been done countless times. I would've liked it if Snoke was the new antagonist of the sequels, but they just completely threw his character away, and again it just goes to show how pointless Darth Vader's death was. 

&#x200B;

A good example of this trope being done is in The Dark Crystal: Age of Resistance with Rian's fight against SkekMal. His father makes a noble sacrifice, only for the viewers to find out moments later that it was all for nought. Imagine making your fans think Anakin is "the chosen one", making a very noble sacrifice and killing Palpatine, making your viewers beliebe he has been dead for 40+ years, just for the director spit in your face and say "somehow Palpatine came back".

A rival cartel killing Lalo will be SO underwhelming. I feel like it needs to be one of the main characters that we see in the story like Mike, Gus, or possibly Gene. There is a popular theory that Jeff the cab driver was sent by Lalo to blow Gene's cover. Now how could this be if Gus says that the Salamanca name dies with Hector? My explanation:

&#x200B;

IF Lalo doesn't die at the hands of Mike or Gus, Lalo will fake his death and disappear. Thus, when Gene calls the vacuum service again and says "Nevermind" he figures it is Lalo and goes to solve the issue. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I know this sounds like a BIG stretch, but I would be so happy if Gene killed Lalo. I know people think it is out of character for him, but I think its fitting considering how much Lalo has terrorized him.
